@@s001 Karatsuba and quicksort
@@s002 dato:digits,ora:product computed,ok:result,no:not needed (the 4th multiplication)
@@s003 I multiply 56 × 78 splitting each number into two digits: <b>a = 5, b = 6</b> (56), <b>c = 7, d = 8</b> (78).
@@s004 Schoolbook: four multiplications: <code>ac, ad, bc, bd</code>. Karatsuba does <b>three</b>. First: ac = 5·7 = 35.
@@s005 bd = 6·8 = 48.
@@s006 The trick: (a+b)(c+d) = 11 · 15 = 165, and <code>ad + bc = 165 − ac − bd = 165 − 83 = 82</code>. Just one multiplication more, and ad + bc comes out without computing them one by one.
@@s007 I recombine: <code>ac·100 + (ad+bc)·10 + bd = 3500 + 820 + 48 = 4368</code> = 56 · 78. Three multiplications of half size: <code>T(n) = 3T(n/2) + O(n)</code>, that is <b>O(n^1.585)</b>.
@@s008 ora:pivot,dato:smaller than the pivot,due:greater or equal,spento:to be fixed
@@s009 Quicksort: I choose a <b>pivot</b>, move the smaller ones to the left and the larger ones to the right, then sort the two parts recursively.
@@s010 I compare each element with the pivot 5. The 3 is smaller: it goes to the left. The 8 is larger: it stays to the right.
@@s011 Result of the partition: <b>3 2 1 4 | 5 | 8 7 6</b>. The pivot is in its final place.
@@s012 Random pivot: on average <b>O(n log n)</b>. With a pivot that is always the worst (the minimum) it becomes O(n²): that is why the pivot is random. Quicksort works in place: little extra memory.
@@s013 Karatsuba and quicksort
@@s014 Two divide and conquer algorithms that beat the naive method: Karatsuba multiplies large numbers with three multiplications instead of four, quicksort sorts with partitions around a pivot.
@@s015 Karatsuba: three multiplications instead of four
@@s016 Multiplying two numbers of <code>n</code> digits at school costs <code>n²</code> (four multiplications of half size). Karatsuba splits each number into two halves: <code>x = a·10^k + b</code>, <code>y = c·10^k + d</code>. It computes <code>ac</code>, <code>bd</code> and <code>(a+b)(c+d)</code>: the mixed term <code>ad + bc</code> comes out by difference, without one more multiplication.
@@s017 Recurrence <code>T(n) = 3T(n/2) + O(n)</code>: <code>O(n^1.585)</code>.
@@s018 56 × 78 with three multiplications
@@s019 Quicksort: partition around the pivot
@@s020 Choose a <b>pivot</b>. Move the smaller elements to the left, the larger (or equal) ones to the right. The pivot is in its final place. Then sort the two parts recursively.
@@s021 With a random pivot the expected cost is <code>O(n log n)</code>. With a pivot that is always the worst (the minimum or the maximum) it becomes <code>O(n²)</code>: that is why the pivot is chosen at random.
@@s022 Partition around the pivot
